                <h2 class="raj-h2"><i data-feather="message-circle" class="raj-h2-icon"></i> Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
                <div class="space-y-4">
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-gray-100">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-800 mb-2">Can heart patients eat sweets during Diwali?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">In most cases yes, in controlled portions, unless your doctor has told you otherwise. A single small piece on a festival day is not what causes harm. Harm comes from a week of unlimited sweets alongside fried snacks, high salt, disrupted sleep and skipped medication. Deciding a fixed number of pieces in advance works far better than attempting complete avoidance and abandoning it by day two.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-gray-100">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-800 mb-2">Why does salt matter more than sugar for blood pressure during festivals?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">Sugar gets the attention but salt moves blood pressure fastest. Festive namkeen, fried snacks, papad, pickles, packaged food and restaurant meals carry far more sodium than home cooking. Extra sodium makes the body retain fluid, raising blood pressure within days and precipitating breathlessness and ankle swelling in anyone with heart failure. Controlling the savoury table usually matters more than the sweets.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-gray-100">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-800 mb-2">What is the best way to eat sweets without a blood sugar spike?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">Eat them immediately after a balanced meal with protein, fibre and vegetables rather than on an empty stomach, because the meal slows absorption. Never skip a meal to save room, since that causes the sharpest spike. Prefer milk or nut-based sweets over deep-fried syrup-soaked ones, drink water rather than sweetened drinks, and take a ten to fifteen minute walk afterwards.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-gray-100">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-800 mb-2">Should diabetics use sugar-free sweets during festivals?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">They help less than people assume. Sugar-free mithai often still contains khoya, ghee, condensed milk and refined flour, supplying calories and fat and still raising blood glucose. The label refers only to added sucrose. Treating them as unlimited usually results in a higher total intake than one normal piece would have. Portion size remains the controlling factor.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-gray-100">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-800 mb-2">What warning signs during the festive week need urgent medical attention?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">Chest discomfort, pressure or heaviness including jaw, neck, arm or upper abdominal pain; breathlessness at rest or lying flat; new foot and ankle swelling; palpitations with dizziness or fainting; severe headache with very high blood pressure; sudden one-sided weakness, facial droop or slurred speech; or blood sugar persistently very high or very low. These are frequently blamed on indigestion, which is how heart attacks get missed.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-gray-100">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-800 mb-2">Is it safe to skip or adjust my medicines during festival travel?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">No. Missed doses are among the most common causes of festive cardiac and diabetic emergencies, usually through travel, disrupted routine or running out of supply. Blood pressure and heart medicines can cause a rebound rise when stopped abruptly. Carry extra, keep tablets in hand luggage, set alarms, and never adjust insulin or diabetes doses around fasting without asking your doctor.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-gray-100">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-800 mb-2">How do fasting days affect diabetic and heart patients?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">Fasting followed by a large evening meal risks low blood sugar during the day and a sharp spike at night. Patients on insulin or sulfonylurea tablets should discuss dose timing before a planned fast rather than after a problem. Hydration during a fast matters too, since dehydration affects blood pressure and kidney function, especially in older patients taking diuretics.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-gray-100">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-800 mb-2">What should a pre-festival health check include?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">Blood pressure measured properly, fasting blood sugar and HbA1c, a lipid profile, kidney function and electrolytes for anyone on blood pressure or diuretic medication, and an ECG for those with existing heart disease or new symptoms. Use the visit to review medicines, arrange enough supply for travel, and get written advice on dose timing around fasting days.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-gray-100">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-800 mb-2">Does festive air pollution affect heart and lung patients?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">Yes. Smoke from fireworks raises particulate levels sharply for several days, and this genuinely worsens asthma, chronic lung disease and angina. Patients with these conditions should keep windows closed on peak nights, stay indoors during the heaviest bursts, keep reliever inhalers to hand, and not dismiss increasing breathlessness as ordinary irritation. Our guide on <a href="/blog/saans-lene-mein-dikkat-ho-to-kya-kare/" class="text-secondary-600 hover:underline font-semibold">what to do when breathing becomes difficult</a> covers the immediate steps.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

